[![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wikimedia_Phabricator_PHP2Shell.png/250px-Wikimedia_Phabricator_PHP2Shell.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Wikimedia_Phabricator_PHP2Shell.png)

[Wikimedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikimedia "Wikimedia")'s [Phabricator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phabricator "Phabricator") instance as seen hours before the patch, disabled as a precaution

**PHP2Shell** is a pair of high-impact [computer vulnerabilities](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vulnerability_\(computer_security\) "Vulnerability (computer security)") (**CVE-2026-109618**[\[2\]](#cite_note-2) and **CVE-2026-109619**[\[3\]](#cite_note-3)) concerning [memory safety](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_safety "Memory safety") flaws in [PHP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PHP "PHP") versions 5\.4–8\.5 that allow [remote code execution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbitrary_code_execution "Arbitrary code execution") (RCE) by a potential malicious user to gain remote control of the [website host](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Website_host "Website host"). The vulnerabilities were discovered with the help of AI in August 2026 and their existence was publicly announced on 20 October 2026\.[\[4\]](#cite_note-oct20-4) An emergency [patch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patch_\(computing\) "Patch (computing)") for versions 8\.2, 8\.3, 8\.4 and 8\.5 and details about the vulnerabilities were released the following day, with numerous websites temporarily taken offline to apply the patches before [exploits](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploit_\(computer_security\) "Exploit (computer security)") (software that uses a security hole to compromise a computer) could be used.[\[5\]](#cite_note-oct21-5)

PHP is a popular programming language on which many [website](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Website "Website") and [web application](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_application "Web application") codebases are built. These vulnerabilities are estimated to affect nearly 70% of [websites](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_wide_web "World wide web").

## Discovery

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=PHP2Shell&action=edit&section=1 "Edit section: Discovery")\]

The vulnerabilities were independently discovered by two parties: Ryan Austin, a researcher at the [California](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California "California")\-based security firm Calif,[\[6\]](#cite_note-6) as part of the [OpenAI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenAI "OpenAI") "Patch the Planet" project in August 2026, and the Israeli–American cloud security company [Wiz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiz,_Inc. "Wiz, Inc."), a subsidiary of [Alphabet Inc.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphabet_Inc. "Alphabet Inc.") and a part of [Google Cloud](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Cloud "Google Cloud").[\[7\.1\]](#cite_note-8) Austin used OpenAI's [GPT-5.6 Sol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GPT-5.6_Sol "GPT-5.6 Sol") model to assist with finding the vulnerability.[\[7\.1\]](#cite_note-8) Wiz researchers used the large language models [Gemini 4 Argon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemini_\(language_model\) "Gemini (language model)") and GPT-5.6 Sol, as well as their own tool, Wiz Atlas, to help with their research.[\[8\]](#cite_note-Satter2026-9) Wiz coined the name "PHP2Shell" indicating that PHP exploits would enable execution of [shell](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shell_\(computing\) "Shell (computing)") commands on affected servers.[\[9\]](#cite_note-10)

PHP versions as early as 5\.4 (released in 2012)[\[10\]](#cite_note-11) were affected by these vulnerabilities, and while concerns around [memory safety](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_safety "Memory safety") bugs had been raised decades before, no action was taken at the time because it was not thought that developing a working [exploit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploit_\(computer_security\) "Exploit (computer security)") could be feasible.[\[11\]](#cite_note-Kan2026-12)[\[7\.2\]](#cite_note-13) A PHP core developer discovered the [out-of-memory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Out-of-memory "Out-of-memory") error behind one of the vulnerabilities during an audit in 2024, but the [bug](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_bug "Software bug") was never fixed; the development team could not reach consensus on how to do so and a specific exploit had not yet been discovered, so the issue was not prioritized.[\[12\]](#cite_note-Constantin2026-14)[\[13\]](#cite_note-15)

The vulnerability was rated 9\.2 (critical) out of 10 under [CVSS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Vulnerability_Scoring_System "Common Vulnerability Scoring System") v4.0 by PHP developers,[\[14\]](#cite_note-16) while security firm watchTowr rated it a full 10\.0.[\[15\]](#cite_note-Abrams-17)

## Patches

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=PHP2Shell&action=edit&section=2 "Edit section: Patches")\]

The maintainers of PHP released patched versions of PHP 8\.2, 8\.3, 8\.4, and 8\.5 (8.2.35, 8\.3.36, 8\.4.27, and 8\.5.12).[\[5\]](#cite_note-oct21-5) [Source code](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source_code "Source code") patches were released for version 7\.4, 8\.0, and 8\.1, but no official updated [binaries](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_file "Binary file") for those versions were released as they are considered [end of life](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_release_life_cycle#End-of-life "Software release life cycle").[\[5\]](#cite_note-oct21-5) Temporary mitigations were also published, but the maintainers said there were no complete mitigations and that updating was the only safe choice.[\[5\]](#cite_note-oct21-5) Both vulnerabilities were published through [GitHub](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GitHub "GitHub") security advisories on 21 October 2026 at 13:34 UTC,[\[16\]](#cite_note-18)[\[17\]](#cite_note-19) 26 minutes before the announced release time, with the PHP website updated shortly thereafter.[\[18\]](#cite_note-20) [cPanel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CPanel "CPanel") and [CloudLinux](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CloudLinux_OS "CloudLinux OS") released backports in their ecosystems.[\[19\]](#cite_note-21)[\[20\]](#cite_note-22) Volunteer maintainer Ondřej Surý, who was given advance access to the fixes by the PHP project, prepared installable packages for old versions from the patched source code so that server administrators could automate deployments without compiling PHP themselves.[\[21\]](#cite_note-23) In response to claims on [X](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X_\(social_network\) "X (social network)") (formerly Twitter) that unpatched older PHP releases left one-third of the Internet permanently vulnerable, Surý stated that there were ways to patch and update the older versions as well.[\[22\]](#cite_note-theatlantic-sury-24)

The PHP maintainers announced the update early and before release, after news of the vulnerabilities leaked on 20 October on the [Hacker News](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hacker_News "Hacker News") forums,[\[23\]](#cite_note-25) in a post on X by cybersecurity company watchTowr labs, and in a [proof-of-concept](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proof-of-concept "Proof-of-concept") exploit video mistakenly released by Calif.[\[7\.3\]](#cite_note-26)[\[12\]](#cite_note-Constantin2026-14) The early notice on PHP.net encouraged users to prepare and update as soon as the patch was released.[\[5\]](#cite_note-oct21-5) One security firm, Sansec, expected "mass attacks to start within 15 minutes of the release".[\[24\]](#cite_note-27) Proof-of-concept exploits, write-ups, and partial mitigations started circulating on the [Internet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet "Internet") before the end of the embargo, after development platform Chainguard released patched versions of the software early; this allowed attackers to determine what code had been changed.[\[15\]](#cite_note-Abrams-17)[\[12\]](#cite_note-Constantin2026-14)

[Amazon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_Web_Services "Amazon Web Services") Threat Intelligence identified a PHP2Shell exploitation attempt dating back to 17 October 2026, in MadPot telemetry, four days before public disclosure.[\[25\]](#cite_note-28) [Cloudflare](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloudflare "Cloudflare") reported signs of early exploitation prior to public [patch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patch_\(computing\) "Patch (computing)") release, and deployed temporary [web application firewall](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_application_firewall "Web application firewall") rules as an attempted stopgap mitigation against the vulnerability.[\[26\]](#cite_note-Gatlan2026-29)[\[27\]](#cite_note-30)[\[28\]](#cite_note-31) Security firm watchTowr said it had observed exploitation in the wild by 21 October through its [honeypot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeypot_\(computing\) "Honeypot (computing)") network,[\[29\]](#cite_note-32) while the [US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Cybersecurity_and_Infrastructure_Security_Agency "US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency") said it had not observed exploitation as of the same day.[\[30\]](#cite_note-Collier2026-33)

## Impact

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=PHP2Shell&action=edit&section=3 "Edit section: Impact")\]

[![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Php2shell_wiki.png/500px-Php2shell_wiki.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Php2shell_wiki.png)

Warning posted on the [English Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Wikipedia "English Wikipedia") about community-owned tools being offline due to the PHP2Shell incident.

The potential impacts of the vulnerability were widespread. Approximately 69% of websites whose server-side programming language is known by [W3Techs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W3Techs?action=edit&redlink=1 "W3Techs (page does not exist)") use PHP, including those that use [content management systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Content_management_system "Content management system") such as [Drupal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drupal "Drupal"), [Joomla](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joomla "Joomla"), and [WordPress](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WordPress "WordPress").[\[30\.1\]](#cite_note-34)[\[31\]](#cite_note-bbc-35) PHP is also bundled with vendor appliances, [web host](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_hosting_service "Web hosting service") control panels such as [cPanel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CPanel "CPanel") and [Plesk](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plesk "Plesk"), and some [Windows](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windows_\(Operating_system\) "Windows (Operating system)") applications.[\[32\]](#cite_note-36)

Following advice from [CERT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_emergency_response_team "Computer emergency response team")\-EU and the [British National Cyber Security Centre](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Cyber_Security_Centre_\(United_Kingdom\) "National Cyber Security Centre (United Kingdom)") (NCSC), many organizations across Europe took their websites offline as a precaution while the patches were pending, including the Dutch government portal Overheid.nl, the Danish national school communication platform, all Finnish government public-facing web services, and all websites of the [French Interior Ministry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_the_Interior_\(France\) "Ministry of the Interior (France)").[\[8\]](#cite_note-Satter2026-9)[\[31\]](#cite_note-bbc-35)[\[33\]](#cite_note-37)[\[34\]](#cite_note-38) Some Canadian government websites were temporarily taken down as a precaution as taken following guidance from the [Canadian Centre for Cyber Security](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canadian_Centre_for_Cyber_Security "Canadian Centre for Cyber Security").[\[35\]](#cite_note-39) The [US National Security Agency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_National_Security_Agency "US National Security Agency") recommended that websites go offline if unable to update immediately.[\[11\]](#cite_note-Kan2026-12)[\[36\]](#cite_note-NSA-40) The NCSC released a similar warning, urging organizations to be wary of [backdoors](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backdoor_\(computing\) "Backdoor (computing)").[\[37\]](#cite_note-41) The Japanese National Cybersecurity Office advised PHP users to immediately update their systems.[\[38\]](#cite_note-ITmedia-21oct2026-42) The [Singaporean Cyber Security Agency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_Security_Agency "Cyber Security Agency") has also urged organizations in [Singapore](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singapore "Singapore") to fix the security flaws.[\[39\]](#cite_note-CNA_i454-43)[\[40\]](#cite_note-CSA_u246-44)

The [Wikimedia Foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikimedia_Foundation "Wikimedia Foundation") shut down many of their supporting services, and [forum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_forum "Internet forum") sites such as [4chan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/4chan "4chan") and [Something Awful](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Something_Awful "Something Awful") went offline.[\[30\]](#cite_note-Collier2026-33)[\[41\]](#cite_note-Tauber-2026-45)[\[11\]](#cite_note-Kan2026-12) Several Japanese websites and applications also went offline, including games such as *[Umamusume: Pretty Derby](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umamusume:_Pretty_Derby "Umamusume: Pretty Derby")*, *[Granblue Fantasy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granblue_Fantasy "Granblue Fantasy")*, *[Princess Connect! Re:Dive](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess_Connect!_Re:Dive "Princess Connect! Re:Dive")*, and numerous [e-commerce](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E-commerce "E-commerce") sites.[\[38\]](#cite_note-ITmedia-21oct2026-42)[\[41\]](#cite_note-Tauber-2026-45) The [Internet Archive](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_Archive "Internet Archive") briefly shut down to fix the issue.[\[42\]](#cite_note-46)

## Analysis

\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=PHP2Shell&action=edit&section=4 "Edit section: Analysis")\]

Researchers at Wiz stated that the issue was on par with [Heartbleed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heartbleed "Heartbleed"), a 2014 vulnerability that affected [OpenSSL](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenSSL "OpenSSL"), an encryption library used by about two-thirds of the web at the time.[\[43\]](#cite_note-47) Coverage in *[The New York Times](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times "The New York Times")* said that PHP was "pervasive" and speculated that "everyday users" could have their personal data put at risk.[\[44\]](#cite_note-48)

Commentary in *[The Guardian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian "The Guardian")* noted that [the PHP Foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_PHP_Foundation "The PHP Foundation"), which maintains the PHP language, only had 13 full- and part-time employees, and relied on financial contributions, whereas AI research, which was credited with helping discover the vulnerability, received "billions" in investment.[\[45\]](#cite_note-49)

[Ubuntu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubuntu "Ubuntu") and [Debian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debian "Debian") developer Ondřej Surý from the Czech Republic who [packages](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Package_manager "Package manager") various PHP tools was credited with playing a crucial role in automating the necessary patches.[\[22\]](#cite_note-theatlantic-sury-24)

